<BaseLayout title="Intake & Air" description="Intake manifold choice, hood clearance, throttle body, and IAT sensor placement for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Intake &amp; Air</h1>
  <p>Hood clearance and radiator airflow drive intake choices as much as power goals.</p>

  <h2>Intake Manifold</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Car intakes</strong> (LS1/LS6/LS3) are the lowest and fit under the hood most easily.</li>
    <li><strong>Truck intakes</strong> are tall, with long runners, and may not clear the Genesis hood. Many builders swap to a car-style or aftermarket manifold.</li>
    <li><strong>FAST 102mm</strong> is a common upgrade. Order the right head-port version: the <em>LSXRT</em> is for cathedral-port truck heads (4.8/5.3/6.0) and the <em>LSXR</em> is for rectangle-port heads (LS3/L92/L99).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="iat">IAT Sensor Placement</h2>
  <p>
    Keep the intake air temp sensor out of hot radiator air. In my car the IAT in the throttle-body coupler read wildly high, so it got moved to the back of the FAST 102, tapped for 3/8" NPT (grind down the tab that's in the way).
  </p>
  <Figure src="iat-sensor-location.jpg" alt="IAT sensor tapped into back of FAST 102 intake manifold" caption="IAT relocated to the back of the FAST 102." />

  <h2>Throttle Body &amp; Pedal</h2>
  <p>
    Drive-by-wire throttle bodies need a matching pedal and an ECU that supports DBW (Holley Terminator X <strong>Max</strong>). Cable throttle works with any setup. See <a href="/guides/gas-pedal">Gas Pedal</a>.
  </p>

